// The peppy framework that runs the robots, as one MCP surface: the calls
// that change the stack of nodes and its halt, not the world and not the
// motion of a robot. Its two targets name daemon interfaces: stack names
// robot_membership:v1, the copies of the launch, and fleet names
// robot_leases:v1, the fleet of the stack, its leases and its halt. The peppy
// daemon that started the server serves them, and no node implements them.
// The launcher scopes each target. The scope of stack gives the robot options
// a client may add, the robot model and the description of each, the features
// of each with their descriptions, and the most robots of these options the
// stack holds, or leaves the target vacant, and the server then serves no
// stack tool. So the document names no robot model, no option and no feature.
// The scope of fleet names the members its client may call, of list, halt and
// clear_halt, and the server serves the tools of these members alone: a
// launcher that keeps the clearance of a halt with the command line leaves
// clear_halt out.
// A robot added here is a copy of its option with the features it names,
// each part on its machine, as `peppy stack join OPTION:NAME@CORE_NODE`
// makes it with one `--with AXIS=OPTION@CORE_NODE` word for each feature,
// the axis and the option that the scope gives that feature; a simulated
// robot's robot_initializer ends its start only once the simulation stands
// it.
//
// The document selects list, halt and clear_halt of robot_leases:v1. The other
// members, the contract documents, the leases, the move check and the
// changes of the fleet, are for programs. A halt is not a motion: it moves
// no robot, and it stops the clients that ask the daemon before they move a
// robot. The leases and the halt are not a safety function.
//
// The launchers hub serves this document through the `robot_control` option
// of each launcher with robots, on port 8903, beside the robots' endpoint on
// port 8900, which drives the robots added here. Its `simulation_mcp`
// launcher scopes the stack target, and the other launchers leave it
// vacant. The title, the opening of the instructions and every description
// say that the calls go to the framework's stack.
{
  peppy_schema: "mcp_exposure/v1",
  manifest: { name: "framework_controls", tag: "v1" },

  server: {
    title: "Robot stack (peppy framework)",
    instructions: "This endpoint controls the peppy framework that runs the robots: the stack of nodes, not the world and not the motion of a robot. Its tools add robots to the stack and remove them, and halt the stack: stack.list reports the robot options this stack offers, the machines of the stack and the robots it can remove, stack.join adds one and stack.remove removes one; fleet.list reports the fleet of the stack, its robots, their leases and the halt, fleet.halt halts the stack and fleet.clear_halt clears the halt; fleet.clear_halt is present only on a stack whose launcher lets this endpoint clear a halt. The stack tools are present only on a stack whose launcher offers robots to add. A robot option can offer features: parts that a robot of that option holds only when it is added with them. A robot and each of its features run on a machine: a peppy daemon, named by its core node name. A feature runs on the robot's machine, with the robot, or on another machine. A robot added here runs the same software as a robot of the same option that the command line adds with the words of the same features. The robots' endpoint drives it under its name, and a simulated robot also stands in the simulated world. Change rule: the stack runs one change at a time, so a call is refused with the failure kind busy while another addition, removal or other change of the stack runs; call again when it has ended. Wait rule: a cancel of a call stops its work at the step it reached, and the stack ends the work as a failure at that step ends it: an addition is undone, and a removal leaves the robot listed with the software it did not stop yet. The call ends cancelled once the stack ended the work. A call that closes before its work is done, by a lost connection too, is cancelled; a task outlives its connection, and only its cancel or its TTL stops its work. A call or a task that hears nothing from the stack for one progress window fails, and its work is cancelled. While the work runs, the change rule refuses another addition or removal, and stack.list reports the work under change. stack.list lists an added robot under copies once its addition has completed. Lease rule: a lease gives one client the right to move one robot. Only the programs of the stack that their launcher allows hold leases; this endpoint holds none. A lease stays after its robot leaves the stack, until its release. stack.join under the name of a robot that another client leases, and stack.remove of a robot that another client leases, are refused with the failure kind leased, and the message names the holder and the lease name. Halt rule: a halt stops the clients that ask the stack before they move a robot: the robots' endpoint, every other MCP server of the stack, the programs that hold leases of robots, and peppy node call. A halt ends every lease. While the stack is halted, the stack refuses each new lease, these clients refuse each call that moves a robot with the failure kind halted, and they cancel each call that they sent that moves a robot. A call that only stops a motion always goes through. A halt is not a motion: it moves no robot and sends no command to a robot, so the safety layer of each robot acts. It does not stop a robot commander, a node that drives its own robot such as a leader arm or the browser panel of a robot, the hand teleoperation, or the setup calls of a simulation: they do not ask the stack. A halt does not change the robots of the stack: stack.join and stack.remove go on, and a robot added during a halt is halted too. The halt stays until it is cleared, with fleet.clear_halt or with peppy fleet clear-halt on the command line, also after a restart of the peppy daemon of the stack. The leases and the halt prevent conflicts between clients; they are not a safety function: the emergency stop of each robot stays in its certified layers.",
  },

  call_record: {
    tool: "stack.recent_calls",
    description: "List the last calls of this endpoint that could change the stack or its halt, newest first, up to 200: every mutating tool, fleet.halt and fleet.clear_halt included, and every action, with started_at, the client that made the call (its declared name and version, or null), the tool, its arguments as sent (null when larger than 1 kB, with arguments_bytes), outcome (running, completed, failed, cancelled or refused), failure_kind, message and duration_ms. A refused call did not change the stack: the change rule refuses a call with failure_kind busy, a name in use, the limit, a feature that is not a feature of its option or a feature named twice for stack.join refuse it with another failure_kind, the lease rule refuses stack.join and stack.remove with failure_kind leased, and arguments outside the schema refuse it with failure_kind null. A failed call gives the failure_kind of its failure, such as attempt_failed or resource_unavailable, and null when it failed without one, such as after a progress window with no word from the stack. message says what ended a call that did not complete: why it was refused or failed, with the log files of a failed change, or the reason of a cancel; it is null for a running or completed call. The record holds the calls of MCP clients on this endpoint only; a robot added to or removed from the stack with peppy on the command line, and a halt or its clearance by another client, are not in it. Takes no arguments and changes nothing.",
    keep: 200,
  },

  targets: {
    // The copies of the launch, served by the peppy daemon within the
    // launcher's scope; a vacant scope leaves the target unserved.
    stack: {
      daemon: { name: "robot_membership", tag: "v1" },
      services: [
        {
          member: "list",
          tool: "stack.list",
          description: "Report what this stack can add and remove, and its machines. options lists each robot option the stack can add: option, the value stack.join takes; model, the model id of a robot of that option, which robot.list on the robots' endpoint reports under identity once the robot is added, and of which scene.preview_asset on the simulated world's endpoint shows a picture as robot/<model>; description, one line that says what a robot of that option is and how it stands; kind, simulated when each part that a robot of that option and its features can bring reads the clock of a simulation, else physical; and features, the features a robot of that option can have, each with feature, the value stack.join takes, and description, one line that says what the feature adds; features is empty when the option has none. core_nodes lists the machines of the stack now: this stack's machine and each live peppy daemon of the same workspace or project, each once, in name order. stack.list reads them on every call, so core_nodes changes while the stack runs: a daemon that starts is in the next answer, and a daemon that stops is not. this_core_node is the machine of this stack. copies lists each robot of these options on the stack, which stack.remove can remove: name, the robot's name; option; core_node, the robot's machine; features, the features of its option that the robot holds, in the order of the option's features, each with feature and core_node, the machine that runs it; and running, true when each part of the robot's software runs. A robot whose core_node, or the core_node of one of its features, is not in core_nodes has software on a machine that is not live now. A robot whose software stopped, for example one that a simulation took out, stays listed with running false until stack.remove removes it. launch_copies lists the robots of these options that the launch of this stack started, each as the launch started it, in the form of an item of copies without its running field: they stay listed whether or not each one is on the stack now, from the launch of the stack until its reset, the next launch or the end of this machine's daemon, and stack.join and stack.remove do not change them; the list is empty after a reset, until the next launch. Each item of launch_copies, and each item of copies with its running field left out, is a valid stack.join input: for a robot that stack.join added, it makes the same robot on the same machines; a robot that the command line added can differ from the robot that its item makes. max_copies is the most robots of these options on the stack: stack.join refuses when the stack holds that many. change is present only while the addition or the removal of a robot of these options runs on the stack, from this endpoint or from any other client: action, join or remove, name, the robot's name, and option. A robot that is being added is in copies only once its addition has succeeded. launcher is the launcher of the stack. Takes no arguments and changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
      ],
      actions: [
        {
          member: "join",
          tool: "stack.join",
          description: "Add a robot of option to the stack under name, with the features it names, and wait until its software runs on each of its machines. Every field is required. name is 1 to 63 letters, digits, '_' or '-', not self, and not the name of a robot on the stack. A simulation can refuse a name by a rule of its own: Waldo refuses two names where one, followed by '_', begins the other, so bravo_2 and bravo cannot stand together. option is one of the options stack.list reports. core_node is the robot's machine, one of the core_nodes stack.list reports, usually this_core_node. features lists the features to add, each with feature, one of the features stack.list reports for option, and core_node, the machine that runs it, one of core_nodes; features is empty for a robot with no feature. Progress messages report each step; building the robot's software on each of its machines, and fetching its files into a simulation, can take minutes the first time. After the call, when it completed, the robot's software runs on the stack, on the robot's machine and on the machine of each feature, and robot.list on the robots' endpoint lists it; a simulated robot stands in the simulation, where the simulation puts a robot that names no spot, and scene.get_robots_list on the simulated world's endpoint lists it with attached true. The call completes with an empty result. An addition that fails ends the call with an error that gives its failure kind, attempt_failed when an add, a build or a start failed and timeout when a step went quiet past its budget, and a message that says why and names the log files, and the addition is undone; when the message says that the copy remains listed, stack.remove removes it. Refused, before the stack changes, with rejected for a feature that is not a feature of option and for a feature named twice, with precondition_failed when the stack holds max_copies robots of the listed options and when a robot on the stack has the name, with leased by the lease rule, and with busy by the change rule. A machine that is not live ends the call with resource_unavailable before the stack changes, and the message lists the live machines. It does not stand a simulated robot at a work surface: workspace.stand_at on the simulated world's endpoint does, and workspace.describe there says whether the robot can work it. See the wait rule and the halt rule.",
          operation: "long_running",
          progress_timeout_ms: 660000,
        },
        {
          member: "remove",
          tool: "stack.remove",
          description: "Remove a robot from the stack, and wait until its software has stopped. name is one of the copies stack.list reports. Progress messages report each part of the robot's software that stops, on each machine that runs it. On a machine that is not live, nothing stops: a progress message warns about it, and that part of the robot's software stays there until the machine's stack is reset. After the call, when it completed, the robot's software has stopped on every live machine of the stack, robot.list on the robots' endpoint no longer lists it, and a call in progress on it has ended with an error. A simulation then takes the robot out of its world: scene.get_robots_list stops listing it once the world is rebuilt without it, and until then the simulation holds the name, so stack.join under the same name can fail; call it again. The call completes with an empty result. A removal that fails ends the call with an error that gives its failure kind and a message that names the log file, and the robot stays listed: stack.remove again stops what is left of it. Refused with rejected for a name stack.list does not report, with leased by the lease rule, and with busy by the change rule. See the wait rule and the halt rule.",
          operation: "long_running",
          progress_timeout_ms: 660000,
        },
      ],
    },
    // The fleet of the stack and its halt, served by the peppy daemon
    // within the launcher's scope.
    fleet: {
      daemon: { name: "robot_leases", tag: "v1" },
      services: [
        {
          member: "list",
          tool: "fleet.list",
          description: "Report the fleet of this stack: its robots, the leases that give one client the right to move one robot, and the halt of the stack. version is the release tag of the peppy that runs the stack, or unknown. core_node is the machine that coordinates the stack, and launcher the launcher of the stack, empty when no launch runs. halted is true while the stack is halted, and halted_by names the client that halted it. robots lists each robot of the stack, in name order: name, the robot's name, the name that the robots' endpoint takes; option, its launcher option; core_node, its machine; kind, simulated when each part of the robot reads the clock of a simulation, else physical; clock, wall or the simulated clock that the robot reads; software, running when each part of the robot's software runs, else stopped; robot_commander, the option of the robot's commander, a node that drives the robot outside the leases and the halt, such as a leader arm or the browser panel, empty when the robot has none; instances, each part of the robot, with its name in the robot, instance (the id the stack runs it under), core_node, node and node_tag; and contracts, each contract that a part of the robot implements, with name, tag, sha256, the hash of its pinned document, and instances, the parts that implement it. leases lists each lease, in robot order: robot; holder and holder_core_node, the client that holds the lease and its machine; name, the name that the holder gave the lease, such as the name of a run; open, true when the holder lets any client move the robot; giver and giver_core_node, the client that gave the lease to its holder, empty for a lease that its holder took; and receiver and receiver_core_node, the client that the holder offers the lease to, empty when no offer runs. A lease stays when its robot leaves the stack, until its release. options lists each robot option and feature that a scope of the stack offers, with kind and the contracts of its parts; documents gives the pinned document of each of these contracts; and nodes lists each node of the stack, with the manifest and the commit that each machine runs, and its instances, each with the robot it belongs to. Takes no arguments and changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
        {
          member: "halt",
          tool: "fleet.halt",
          description: "Halt the stack: the stack ends every lease, a lease that its holder received included, with no give-back to its giver. After the call, the stack is halted: fleet.list reports halted true, and the halt rule holds until fleet.clear_halt. A call while the stack is halted succeeds and changes nothing. The call does not wait for the clients: it does not wait until they have cancelled their calls or until a robot stands still.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
        {
          member: "clear_halt",
          tool: "fleet.clear_halt",
          description: "Clear the halt of the stack. After the call, the stack is not halted: fleet.list reports halted false, the stack grants leases again, and the clients that ask the stack let calls that move a robot go through again. It starts nothing again: no call that the halt cancelled runs again, and no lease that the halt ended comes back; each client takes its leases again. A call while the stack is not halted succeeds and changes nothing. It does not check that a robot can move safely.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
      ],
    },
  },
}
